#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088653} :activity/id #uuid "aa1e5312-523b-58c8-969d-8407a1c3ddec" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050378 :topic/id #uuid "44594723-00b7-53c1-8509-39704843a698" :topic/math-academy-id 883} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088950 :tutorial/content "Recall that second-order inhomogeneous differential equation takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only and $f(x)$ is a nonzero function.\n\nIn general, we say that a second-order ODE has **sinusoidal forcing** if $f(x)$ is a sinusoidal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the ODE below has sinusoidal forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y= 2 \\cos 2 x\n$$\n\nSolving a second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing is similar to the method for solving a second-order ODE with polynomial forcing. The general solution is still the sum of the complementary solution $y_c$ (i.e., the solution to the associated homogeneous ODE) and a particular solution $y_p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nThe only difference is in how we find a particular solution. Given a second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing $f(x) = a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x,$ provided that $\\sin \\omega x$ and $\\cos \\omega x$ do not feature in the complementary solution, then we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha \\cos \\omega x + \\beta  \\sin \\omega x.\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are unknown constants to be determined." :tutorial/id #uuid "c922743e-1a32-51dd-a109-feac1b1930c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4066 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91f805b4-d536-567b-92f7-524437b74146" :step/math-academy-id 24264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087606 :question/id #uuid "c25573a9-f51a-5cf2-aa32-a343e96dd732" :question/math-academy-id "e-6612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17b31ef7-a3d0-5315-8dbd-e92eafcce17f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433401}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Differential Equation Using Sinusoidal Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "fcefea54-b58a-521e-b82e-14299e2f316d" :step/math-academy-id 24265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088951 :tutorial/content "Given a second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing $f(x) = a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x,$ if $\\cos \\omega x$ or $\\sin \\omega x$ *is* a term in the complementary solution, then $a \\cos \\omega x + b \\sin \\omega x$ *cannot* be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x(\\alpha \\cos \\omega x + \\beta  \\sin \\omega x).\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + y= 4 \\cos x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = \\textrm{i}$ or $\\lambda =  -\\textrm{i}.$ Consequently, the complimentary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos x + B \\sin x.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $4 \\cos x$ and $\\cos x$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta \\sin x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x), \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = 0$ and $\\beta = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 2x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "703ca0f2-aa95-5326-811b-83d5a458ccd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4067 :tutorial/title "The Case When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution Have the Same Term"} :step/id #uuid "38062346-4103-5ff0-815d-26c1948b8d40" :step/math-academy-id 24266 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087607 :question/id #uuid "b11bf2d1-c79a-5308-bcda-109d217b14cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6613"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433399}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"} :step/id #uuid "ecd022a7-62c1-51a9-8e53-2f732b860fdf" :step/math-academy-id 24267 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088663}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088955 :tutorial/content "Let's solve the following second-order ODE with sinusoidal forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y= 2 \\cos 2 x\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 1$ or $\\lambda = -2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $2 \\cos 2x,$ and $\\cos 2x$ does not appear as a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha \\cos 2x + \\beta  \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ (2\\beta -6\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-6\\beta -2\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding terms on both sides should have the same coefficients. Equating the coefficients of like terms, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -6\\alpha =2 \\\\ -6\\beta -2\\alpha =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, it follows that \n\n$$\n- 6 \\beta - 2 \\alpha = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\alpha = - 3 \\beta.\n$$\n\n Substituting $\\alpha=-3\\beta$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -6(-3)\\beta  & =2 \\\\ 20\\beta  & =2 \\\\ \\beta  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting into $\\alpha = -3\\beta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -3(0.1) = -0.3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = - 0.3 \\cos 2x + 0.1  \\sin 2x,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-0.3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+0.1\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9ba5d11b-8158-56a5-9e90-b52297ff5938" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4139 :tutorial/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Sinusoidal Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "0a04129d-2ef1-51c2-a987-dc643edb1e96" :step/math-academy-id 25051 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087615 :question/id #uuid "a23b7e25-1876-5a71-a638-18b5d548bd16" :question/math-academy-id "e-6788"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049099}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357579}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "289c0c9c-bc55-5a0e-a317-ab36694e1a6d" :step/math-academy-id 25098}] :activity/title "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Sinusoidal Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087948}]}
